WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.019 --> 00:00:12.480
When you think of electricity, what's the
first thing that comes to mind?

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.970
Probably a wire. We're used to thinking of
metals as the best way to conduct

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:18.500
electric current.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.599
And it's true--metals are found in pretty
much all the electronics that we use:

00:00:22.599 --> 00:00:26.009
in light bulbs, phones, computers, TVs,

00:00:26.009 --> 00:00:30.759
and countless other appliances. But believe it or not, there are certain non-metals,

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:34.050
such as conductive plastics that can act
as conductors, too.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.640
Their special properties challenges our
assumptions about non-metals,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.820
and allow them to harness the power of
electricity. Although electricity can

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:43.800
seem like magic,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.480
the science behind it is pretty simple. An electric current is just the flow of

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:49.329
electrons from

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:54.050
atom-to-atom. Different atoms are better
at connecting electrons than others

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:58.000
which is why materials like metals are
generally more conductive than others.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.850
This is because the electrons metals are
delocalized,

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.700
meaning that the outermost electrons in
a metal atom are held loosely,

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.930
allowing them to flow more freely. In
contrast,

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:12.569
the outer electrons in the atoms of a non-
metal are held more closely to the

00:01:12.569 --> 00:01:13.280
nucleus,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.439
preventing movement. Think about an
electrical wire.

00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:19.990
Inside, the charges flow easily through the metal filaments.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:24.029
On the outside, the rubber coating keeps 
these charges from flowing into our

00:01:24.029 --> 00:01:24.569
bodies

00:01:24.569 --> 00:01:29.529
and electrocuting us when we touch the
wire. But electrically-conductive plastics

00:01:29.529 --> 00:01:30.810
turn this idea on its head.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:35.100
These plastics are polymers, long
chains made of smaller,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.849
repeating units known as monomers. By
changing the structure of

00:01:38.849 --> 00:01:42.450
the atoms within the monomers, these
plastics can actually become

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.789
just as conductive as certain metals. Sounds pretty cool,

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:50.109
right? You may think that some brilliant
chemist made these plastic specifically

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.090
for this purpose.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.209
But, like so many other important
discoveries in science,

00:01:54.209 --> 00:01:58.990
this one was an accident. It happened in the Lab of Hideki Shirakawa,

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.539
when the chemical compound polyacetylene 
was mixed incorrectly.

00:02:02.539 --> 00:02:06.409
Normally black and powdery, the compound
instead formed a silvery

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:10.640
film that looked distinctly metallic. Wondering if this strange substance would

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.180
exhibit metallic properties,

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.840
Shirakawa shared the discovery with chemist
Alan MacDiarmid

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.750
and physicist Alan Heeger. After
experimenting with many different

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:20.650
processes,

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:24.290
they discovered that they could actually
increase the conductivity

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.190
of polyactylene to rival that of certain
metals. With the addition of bromine gas,

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.020
for instance,

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:34.010
the conductivity of polyacetylene
increased 10 million--fold--

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:38.910
to a level that approached that copper. Although a different conductive polymer,

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:42.180
polyaniline, had existed for almost two
centuries,

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.730
the accidental discovery of polyacetylene
brought the field of conductive polymers

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:50.410
to the forefront and opened up a
revolutionary new possibilities for

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:51.570
experimentation.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:55.610
While metals are generally the best
conductors we have available today,

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:58.620
there are many downsides to their use. Mining,

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.800
shipping, and processing metals is
expensive. Conductive polymers

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.020
are attractive alternatives because
they are lightweight, cheap

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:10.290
and functionally versatile. So how do these conductive polymers work?

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.270
Like in metals, the flow of electric
charge in conducting polymers is

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:15.640
generated by voltage--

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.360
a difference in the electrical potential
the negative and positive poles of a

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:19.900
battery

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:23.720
or an outlet. The negatively charged
electrons are drawn towards the positive

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:24.410
pole,

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:28.720
and they create a current as they pass from atom-to-atom. But like we mentioned,

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.100
the electron of polymers usually aren't
delocalized, and cannot easily move around

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:33.970
to conduct current.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:37.240
But let's take another look at polyacetylene.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.250
if you look closely, you'll notice that
there's an alternating pattern of single

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.120
and double bonds holding the atoms of the polymer together.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.450
This is known as a conjugated backbone. Along this backbone,

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:53.230
the sigma bonds hold each atom together by
concentrating electrons directly between them.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:57.450
The pi bonds, which constitute the
second bond in each double bond,

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:01.569
strengthen the connection between their
atoms by attracting electrons above and

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:03.250
below the plane and the molecule.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:07.580
This forms delocalized orbitals,
where the mobility of electrons can be

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:08.350
enhanced

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:13.300
through a process called "doping." This process
essentially changes the number of

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.600
electrons in the polymer,

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:19.199
by either removing or adding electrons to the
atoms. Removing electrons

00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:22.270
create empty spaces in the outermost
orbital the atom,

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:25.460
allowing the remaining electrons to
move around more freely.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:29.210
Adding electrons forces an atom to
create another orbital--

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:33.189
and so long as this orbital isn't full, the electrons have more space to

00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:33.870
move around

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:39.479
and hop from atom-to-atom. You can think of it 
like a pool table full of billiard balls.

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:43.090
The balls would be able to move around, if they weren't getting in each other's way.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:46.310
But if you take out a few balls, the
rest of them can move

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.860
around. Or alternately, if you add more
balls,

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.300
you need to add a new pool table. In this new pool
table,

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:57.249
they have lots of room to move around. The more easily they can move around,

00:04:57.249 --> 00:05:01.960
the more easily they can transport
charges and conduct electrical currents.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:05.699
Although initially no one believed that
polymers could conduct electricity,

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:09.469
the scientific community has since cut
up to Shirakawa's research.

00:05:09.469 --> 00:05:12.509
In 2000, Shirakawa, MacDiarmid

00:05:12.509 --> 00:05:15.749
and Heeger were awarded the Nobel
Prize in Chemistry--

00:05:15.749 --> 00:05:19.409
and the research around conductive
polymers has grow exponentially

00:05:19.409 --> 00:05:20.840
 

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.439
While the full potential of conductive
polymers has yet to be discovered,

00:05:24.439 --> 00:05:28.559
there are countless possibilities for
developments in technology and medical since,

00:05:28.559 --> 00:05:33.240
such as high-capacity batteries, artificial
muscles, and biosensors.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.689
So while we probably won't see plastics
entirely replacing metals in our

00:05:36.689 --> 00:05:37.620
lifetime,

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.620
the field of conductive polymers has huge
potential,

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:46.840
especially for something that began as a
mistake.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.800
 

